===Etymology===
1835 ''Kanuk'' (US), 1849 ''canuck'' (Canadian), origin {{unk.}}
{{rel-top|theories}}
Several dictionaries simply state that it is an alteration of {{term|Canada}} or {{term|Canadian}}.  More than one theory holds that the name began as an informal self-appellation by an early Canadian minority, and later acquired a national identity. A few sources explain the ending as coming from {{etyl|iu}} {{term|inuk||man, person|lang=iu}}, from {{term|Chinook||Aboriginal people of the U.S. Pacific Northwest}}, or another Canadian Aboriginal ending like {{term||-uc}}, {{term||-uq}}, or {{term||-oc}}.

Another theory is that the name is from the surname {{term|Connaught||lang=fr}}, used as a French-Canadian nickname for the Irish.  Yet another speculates that the origin is {{etyl|lre}} {{term|kanata||village|lang=lre}}, which is also the origin of {{term|Canada}}.  It has also been thought to come from {{etyl:iro}} {{term|Canuchasa||hut}}, {{etyl|de}} {{term||[[Genug]] von Canada|enough of Canada|lang=de}}, or {{etyl|fr}} {{term||[[quelle]] [[canule]]|lang=fr}}.

Since 1975, a number of linguists have come to believe that the name probably comes from {{etyl|haw}} {{term|kanaka||man|lang=haw}}, a self-appellation of indentured colonial [[canoeman|canoemen]] and Hawaiian sailors working off the Pacific Northwest, Arctic, and New England coasts.  The term may have come to English through {{etyl|fr}} {{term|canaque|lang=fr}}, or more likely, via American [[whalers]].  Compare English {{term|Kanak}} and French {{term|Kanak|lang=fr}} or {{term||canaque|black person|lang=fr}}, Austrian German {{term|Kanake|lang=de}}.
{{rel-bottom}}

